  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1156 AM MDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nTwo synoptic scale features will continue to exert their\ninfluence on local weather this week. Cyclonic flow in the \nPACNW will allow some cooler air to moderate temperatures \nacross the Great Basin, while a blocking ridge amplifies over\nthe central Canadian provinces. This is all evolving beneath a\njet streak that stretches from Baja across the Four Corners onto\nthe Great Plains. While new moisture remains absent from the\nequation, remnant surface moisture from recent rainfall will get\nreworked today, and to a lesser extent Wednesday, to produce a \nfew thunderstorms and showers. A few of these storms could\nsustain strong showers. A marginal risk for flash flooding\ncontinues this afternoon for flood prone areas, subject to \nheavy rainfall. As the previously mentioned jet begins to nose\ninto northeast UT and northwest CO this evening, a few showers\ncould get a lease on life into the overnight hours favoring\nareas north of I-70. While models remain ambivalent to overnight\nshowers, it does appear that this jet streak overhead will keep\nsome threat of light rain in the forecast into Wednesday\nmorning.\n\nDrier air approaches from the west on Wednesday, but some\nremnant moisture beneath the strong jet streak will get another\nchance to queue up some storms Wednesday afternoon. Strong\nsteering winds will keep storm motions fast, thus reducing the\nflash flooding risk Wednesday. Gusty outflow winds will remain a\nthreat. Those stronger winds aloft will mix down to the surface\nWednesday afternoon with gusts to around 35 mph expected across\nthe region. A few sheltered valleys will see lighter winds, but\neverywhere will feel breezier...perhaps another hint of \nautumn's arrival. Drier air continues to invade Thursday and \nwinds remain breezy. Shower activity will taper Thursday,\nallowing for some reprieve in rainfall.\n\nHoliday Weekend and Beyond\n\nThe deepening PACNW cyclone is projected to amplify high\npressure over the central CONUS this weekend. As the high \nbuilds westward, models are hinting at another monsoonal fetch \ndeveloping, potentially including some richer tropical storm\nmoisture. This could send some heavy showers back into the Four\nCorners Friday and Saturday. Given the concern for flash \nflooding in the Southwest this time of year, weekend trekkers \nthat are venturing into the slot canyons and onto the mountains \nwill want to keep abreast of the forecast, given the increased \nchance for thunderstorms. Models attempt to eject the low across\nthe Great Basin Monday. This could produce a wet Labor Day \nforecast to close out the weekend. This is a developing forecast\nand will command some attention as it evolves.",
